{"archetypes":[{"name":"kv-contract-tracer","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The plan wires kv_value SKIP_REASON from generator stdout into step-7a.sh but never verifies the emit side of the contract or the parsing semantics of kv_value.","prompt_body":"Trace the SKIP_REASON KV chain end-to-end: verify that generate-code-flow-diagram.sh actually emits a `SKIP_REASON=` line to stdout (not stderr or a sidecar file) in every status=skipped and status=failed branch. Check the kv_value helper's documented parsing contract — specifically whether it reads from a string argument or a stream, whether it handles multiword values, and whether gen_out could be empty or truncated before kv_value is called. Confirm the wildcard branch's rationale ('gen_status not returned so SKIP_REASON is not trustworthy') is consistent with how gen_out is captured — if gen_out is captured regardless of gen_status, the wildcard could silently discard a valid SKIP_REASON. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"harness-drift-auditor","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The plan updates test-step-7a.sh assertions for SKIP_REASON cases but describes the process as 'iterate the file and fix mismatches one at a time' without enumerating which specific test cases require changes, risking silent under-coverage.","prompt_body":"Audit the plan's test-step-7a.sh assertion update strategy: identify every `new_case` invocation in test-step-7a.sh that sets STEP7A_GEN_FORCE_SKIP_REASON (or equivalent fixture env) and verify each is explicitly named in the plan's Item A/B coupling section. Check whether the plan's claim that `diagram-rejected-<token>` loop cases set a per-token SKIP_REASON is consistent with the actual loop structure in test-step-7a.sh — if the loop iterates tokens but the fixture env is set once outside the loop, the assertion update strategy is wrong. Verify the plan's assertion that `diagram-rejected` baseline and `generator-crash` do NOT set SKIP_REASON, so they correctly test the fallback path. Flag any case where the plan says 'keep the placeholder assertion' but the fixture actually does set a SKIP_REASON env var. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"sanitizer-byte-scope","focus_area":"security","weight":1,"rationale":"The plan uses tr -d '[:cntrl:]' which strips tabs and other bytes that may appear legitimately in diagnostic output, but the concrete fallback mitigation (narrowing the strip set) is only mentioned in Failure Mode 2 prose, not in any specific edit spec.","prompt_body":"Check whether the plan's concrete edit specs for lib-quiet.sh and ci-failed-jobs.sh specify the exact byte class to strip (`[:cntrl:]` vs a narrower set), or whether the implementer must infer it from a buried Failure Mode prose paragraph. Verify that existing callers of larch_err in lib-quiet.sh, test-lib-quiet.sh, and ci-failed-jobs.sh pass content containing literal tab characters (`\t`) in any test fixtures or expected-output strings — if they do, the plan's acknowledged risk of stripping tabs becomes a concrete harness regression, not a theoretical one. Check whether the plan instructs the implementer to run test-lib-quiet.sh before landing Item D changes that depend on the sanitized larch_err behavior, or whether the ordering leaves a window where ci-failed-jobs.sh double-sanitization could behave differently than expected if lib-quiet.sh is not yet updated. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
